    /// <summary>
    /// MLEDIT's tees (CT, OT, MT) of a stem of one or two segments onto a second of two or three, against the records AutoCAD
    /// 2021 left (accoreconsole, read back through <c>DxfOut</c> of a <c>Wblock</c>), STANDARD at scale 20 justified at the
    /// top: the meeting is taken from every pair of segments whose lines meet, the stem met on a segment or run on past its
    /// free end, the second met on a segment or run on past its own free end, the pair nearest the two picks along the
    /// multilines (the distances added), and that pair alone says whether the tee is made (the second met on a segment) or
    /// is <c>These multilines do not intersect.</c> (met past its free end); where there is no such pair, the tee is
    /// <c>Invalid operation on a self intersecting multiline.</c> if any pair has the stem run on past its free end to a second
    /// met past a vertex of its own on an end segment, or the stem met past a vertex of its own and the second past its free
    /// end, and <c>These multilines do not intersect.</c> if none has. Each element of the stem stops at the second's near
    /// element wherever that is met, on the piece of its mitred path nearest the segment of the pair. Rows are the option, the
    /// points of the stem, those of the second, where each is picked and what AutoCAD answered, as
    /// <see cref="MlineTeePairTests"/> writes them; rows whose picks lie within 10 of the other multiline are left out
    /// (AutoCAD may take the other multiline for the pick); the 30 rows where the stem and the second run along one another
    /// (two pairs as near as each other) show the order of such ties, which agrees with AutoCAD in 334 of the 356 probed.
    /// </summary>
    public class MlineTeeThreeSegmentTests
